db/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161410 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f1b8eeaf-e0a8-51a8-9620-e128242196f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161411 #edb/ref 1080 "0.4879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161411 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161411 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f2c4a4b4-e329-5925-978d-4fe4dac9ad95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161412 #edb/ref 1080 "0.4214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161412 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161412 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "711756fd-4f99-501c-bab1-c06f4c3f7cf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161413 #edb/ref 1080 "0.4547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161413 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161413 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "740bd59c-6369-5240-8c7d-81a21205d86b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161745 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16584" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161745 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(1\\right),$ calculate $P\\left(X < 2\\right).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161745 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d3ad56f-a242-5335-aa48-e2608b1b973d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161745 #edb/ref 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/f3/f3440005b2b4e7ce1cc2eed5825d11cb309522a6807bcd18ffada189fadd0cda.svg)\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( 1 \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/1f/1f2f28b0f36aeb85a07076e1507c9561b707bf7d00de727998b02075b5458da4.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P\\left(X < 2\\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-x}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161746 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161746 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(\\dfrac 23\\right),$ calculate $P\\left(X \\geq 3\\right).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161746 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1adfc5c-78aa-5f14-bec5-cf763d86e5af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161746 #edb/ref 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/49/49fa146efc8029727469bc5909c2a946c9cb96383976d4096d455b423a428479.svg)\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac 23 \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/72/721ca1810b816431f99bb8294f4ad12ff125342640a925035487c8ecace4eff6.svg)Therefore, we can calculate $P\\left(X \\geq 3\\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2x/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-2x/3}|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1353\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161747 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161747 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(2\\right),$ calculate $P\\left(\\dfrac 12 < X < 2\\right).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161747 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fffe6dba-c0a1-5644-a975-6d51b6de2f98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161747 #edb/ref 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/e7/e77daa45c1026175eec99e71cb336ad5b8c2a46683170b0f9357a4ad7e9d1bf5.svg)\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( 2 \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/e0/e0b6d1388350fabce97f26601d512e60cfe6ac3ab41230671718ca034c5c3860.svg)Therefore, we can calculate $P\\left(\\dfrac 12 < X < 2\\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{2}<X<2) & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}2{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-2x}|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4}+{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ≈0.3496\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161748 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161748 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(4\\right),$ calculate $P\\left(X \\leq 7 \\, \\bigg |\\, X > 5\\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161748 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88232e58-999e-5ac8-8f09-8a4b204ec51c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161748 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ and two positive real numbers $s, t,$ the memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big |\\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\leq s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = 1-P(X>s+t \\, \\big |\\, X>s) = 1-e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda = 4,$ $s=5,$ and $s+t=7.$ From this, we can find $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt = (t+s) - s =7 - 5 = 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7\\,|\\,X>5) & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(4)\\cdot (2)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-8} \\\\  & ≈0.999\\,664.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161930 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161930 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a \"Less Than\" Probability With the Exponential Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161930 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161930 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1cc5827c-739e-52fd-9f10-a75bf2c18c22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161931 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161931 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161931 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161931 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161931 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a \"Greater Than\" Probability With the Exponential Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161931 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161931 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a1677dd6-74e4-5778-bbba-fbb230c7646a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161932 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161932 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Probability on a Bounded Interval Using the Exponential Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161932 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161932 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7c89beba-5605-514e-82c3-8e8145b178c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161933 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161933 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161933 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161933 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161933 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "aab06631-cd2c-57d2-9518-25f7a31c4b6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1045 "The Exponential Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162203 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "50fb9a01-de8e-5ba2-ab6c-78aa8023faaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162685 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162685 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162685 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162685 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(2),$ calculate $P(X<\\frac{1}{6}).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162685 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45110329-7387-51ad-ab6c-5c96036db67b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162685 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162685 #edb/ref 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(2).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X<\\frac{1}{6})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<\\frac{1}{6}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/6}2{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}{|}_{0}^{1/6} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1/3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.2835\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162686 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162686 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162686 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162686 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ calculate $P(1<X<4).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162686 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bac13043-3683-5332-9a90-e9dca4fb305d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162686 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162686 #edb/ref 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(1<X<4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<4) & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.4712\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162757 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162757 #edb/ref 1061 224790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162757 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7a402afc-6129-5cac-b4c5-dce09e83e3f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162757 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162758 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162758 #edb/ref 1061 224791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162758 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "57a0fac0-7935-51d2-8db4-40cd4f4ff7e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162758 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162759 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162759 #edb/ref 1061 224794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162759 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aaebd855-e8da-5148-bcca-168a8644d4fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162759 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162760 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162760 #edb/ref 1061 224797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162760 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32e9215c-bd3d-5527-bbf4-9e6f37a98e5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162760 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162761 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162761 #edb/ref 1061 224800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162761 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2894872e-ce40-5e6f-a5b3-1261d99d3e24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162761 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162762 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162762 #edb/ref 1061 224801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162762 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a4bba0d-64ae-53f7-b86a-7458bdc9351d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162762 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162763 #edb/ref 1061 224802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a238cab-5690-5d48-8149-4d2eee2010b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163182 #edb/ref 1064 "A continuous random variable $X$ follows an **exponential distribution** if it has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/2a/2ae9fdb32ab1b90d11729ead5966100d53b722ce182eacfb58ff9dc7d037993f.svg)\n\nThe **rate parameter** $\\lambda$ is a positive real number, and $e\\approx 2.71828$ is Euler's number. \n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows an exponential distribution, we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda).\n$$\n\nThe exponential distribution models the amount of continuous space or time between two events. In a future lesson, we will learn how to model situations using the exponential distribution. But for now, let's get some practice working with the PDF to compute probabilities." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163182 #edb/ref 1065 10784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163182 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c2a50890-7334-508e-bac0-19d71ccce3db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163182 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163183 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda),$ and two positive real numbers $s, t.$ \n\nSuppose we're given that  $X>s,$ and we want to determine the probability that $X>s+t.$ In other words, we wish to compute the conditional probability \n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\,\\big|\\, X>s).\n$$\n\n \n\nBy the definition of conditional probability, we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = \\dfrac{P\\big((X> s+t) \\cap (X > s)\\big)}{P(X>s)}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $X>s+t$ implies that $X>s,$ therefore\n\n$$\nP\\big((X> s+t) \\cap (X > s)\\big) = P(X > s+t).\n$$\n\nSo, the above conditional probability simplifies to\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\,\\big|\\, X>s) = \\dfrac{P(X> s+t )}{P(X>s)}.\n$$\n\nNext, let's recall how to calculate $P(X > a)$ for some $a>0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>a) & =1-P(X<a) \\\\  & =1-{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+{{e}^{-\\lambda x}|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{-\\lambda a}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can use this fact to calculate $P(X> s+t )$ and $P(X>s)$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s) & =\\frac{P(X>s+t)}{P(X>s)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\lambda (s+t)}}{{e}^{-\\lambda s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\lambda s}\\cdot {e}^{-\\lambda t}}{{e}^{-\\lambda s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\lambda s}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\lambda t}}{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\lambda s}}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that $e^{-\\lambda t} = P(X > t).$ So, we have proved the following property:\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\big| X>s) = P(X > t).\n$$\n\nThis property is called **memoryless property.** It can be proved that the exponential distribution is the only continuous distribution with this property (note that the geometric distribution is the only *discrete* distribution with the memoryless property).\n\nIntuitively, the memoryless property states that the probability of an event occurring after a certain amount of time has already elapsed is independent of how much time has passed. This means that the distribution \"forgets\" what has happened before time $s.$ \n\nWe'll discuss how the memoryless property is used for mathematical modeling in future lessons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163183 #edb/ref 1065 10785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163183 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "02202f83-1511-550a-ab05-645cfdda4003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163183 #edb/ref 1067 "The Memoryless Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163185 #edb/ref 1064 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(2),$ let's calculate the following probability:\n\n$$\nP\\left(X > \\dfrac32 \\,\\bigg|\\, X > \\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nGiven a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ and two positive real numbers $s, t,$ the memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda = 2, \\qquad s=\\dfrac 12,\\qquad s+t=\\dfrac32.\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can find $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt = (t+s) - s = \\dfrac32-\\dfrac12 = 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>\\frac{3}{2}\\,|\\,X>\\frac{1}{2}) & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(2)\\cdot (1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & ≈0.135\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163185 #edb/ref 1065 10800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163185 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "91736de3-87f0-59ed-839b-e2d12dc5744d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163185 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423964 #edb/ref 1080 "1-3{e}^{-2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423964 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423964 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00532a77-8369-5069-a645-da39692238cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423965 #edb/ref 1080 "0.3654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423965 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423965 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09a50814-795d-5d28-8e92-209754c2af10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423966 #edb/ref 1080 "0.3611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423966 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423966 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "20637901-6ccf-5b0b-8845-93cfcc926093" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423967 #edb/ref 1080 "0.4150" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423967 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423967 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a753c6c-3899-5081-81c1-9c51956a63b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423968 #edb/ref 1080 "0.2231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423968 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423968 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2c1745b6-630b-529f-9136-c40f62c94d25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423969 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{{e}^{8}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423969 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423969 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31d5edd8-2b29-5eba-8a70-917a5b0f20f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218642397